<h2>Extracting a design system</h2>
<p>When building a web service, developers usually receive a <strong>design system</strong> made by a designer and build screens on top of it. With Claude Code you can run this process <strong>in reverse</strong> — pull just the design elements out of a site that already exists and turn them into a design system.</p>

<h3>Requesting the extraction</h3>
<pre><code>Extract the design elements of this finished site
and build a separate design system from them.</code></pre>

<h3>What gets captured</h3>
<p>It pulls only the design elements of the current site and defines them in a single document.</p>
<ul>
    <li>Color palette</li>
    <li>Text (typography) styles</li>
    <li>Components — button styles, layout styles</li>
    <li>Animation effects</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why build one</h3>
<p>Once you have a design system, you can <strong>keep stamping out future sites with the same design</strong>. It keeps the tone consistent when you develop an internal system or build a second or third site, and it is easy to hand over and share with colleagues.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Next step</span>
    You can reuse a design system built this way as it is, but if you plan to "keep using it from now on," it is more convenient to turn it into a <strong>Skill</strong>. (Covered in the next document.)
</div>
